Z||000006||Kasdan||10-13-2001||07:26 PM||||Any way this can be a flywheeel problem?  or the pins in the tranny being worn?<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">           <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||10-13-2001||08:13 PM||||No, that's a ratio fault. The tranny is engaging gears that it shouldn't be. I suspect a valve-body fault. Could be as simple as a few solenoids went berserk. They ARE electronically actuated trannies, after all.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000008||sluppy123||10-13-2001||11:35 PM||||Most likely it's inside the tranny, possibly a solenoid in the valve-body. The transfer case would have nothing to with this type of problem, it just re-directs the power from the tranny to the front and rear wheels.<P>Just so you know you have a 42RE 4 speed automatic. What 4x4 system do you have? command trac, select-a-trac?<P>I've got a 93 grand with all kinds of goodies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>                <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=54316496&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Kasdan||10-13-2001||11:36 PM||||umm, its a laredo 4x4, it has 2wd, 4wd high, and 4wd low, all part time of course.||||reg||1
Z||000010||MrPBody||10-14-2001||12:48 AM||||Sorry, I have no clue; our '88 4.0 has a 5-speed.  Throttle Position Sensors are notorious for causing strange automatic transmisson problems, but I doubt it's causing this one.<P>Try the <A HREF="http://www.jeepsunlimited.com/" TARGET=_blank>Jeeps Unlimited</A> bulletin board, or <A HREF="http://www.naxja.org/" TARGET=_blank>NAXJA</A>.<P>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
